<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xavante</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as A'uwe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xavante are a Gê-speaking indigenous people of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In the 18th century they lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a>, where they clashed with settlers and some were gathered into government villages, before moving west into Mato Grosso. They resisted contact with Brazilian society until the 1940s and now live on reserves in eastern Mato Grosso.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xavante.jpg" alt="Xavante" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clockwise from top left: Roosewelt Pinheiro, Márcio Meira, Márcio Meira, Elza Fiúza, Roosewelt Pinheiro, Valter Campanato (all ABr … · CC BY 3.0 br · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xavante.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
